In this experiment, I learned how to monitor AI model usage and calculate the estimated cost of generating responses. The main aim was to collect information about the AI model whenever a user sends a prompt. First, I prepared the Python files required for the experiment, including llm_client.py, cost_calculator.py, mongo_logger.py, and main.py. I used Gemini and Ollama as the two available AI providers. After selecting a provider, I entered a prompt, and the selected model generated a response. Along with the response, the program collected details such as input tokens, output tokens, total tokens, response time, and estimated cost. The cost calculator was used to estimate the API cost based on the number of tokens and the configured pricing values. I also used MongoDB to store the information about each model request. The MongoDB database was named llm_monitoring, and the collection used was call_logs. Whenever a successful request was completed, the program stored the provider name, model name, prompt, token usage, response time, and other details in the database. I also understood how retry handling works when temporary errors occur during API communication. After completing the setup, I executed main.py and checked the generated response and the saved monitoring information. Through this experiment, I learned that monitoring is important when developing AI applications because it helps us understand model usage, response time, and estimated API expenses.
